  "query_preview": "..\n\nAloft, the upper level ridge will migrate north and east from\nits previous Four Corners position into the central to northern\nplains today. While this shift will weaken winds aloft and\ntherefore the wind shear that has enhanced thunderstorm severity\nthis past weekend, moist southeasterly flow will persist today.\nAmple atmospheric moisture characterized by 1.2-1.7 inches of \nprecipitable water and 50-60 degree dew points will be in place \ntoday and given adequate surface heating, another round of \nscattered thunderstorms is likely this afternoon and evening. \nWith 0-6km shear becoming weaker and under 15 kts, the severe \nwind threat should be lower with heavy rainfall rates providing \nthe primary hazard with any thunderstorm. Locations under the \nstrongest cells may see rainfall totals of 1-1.5 inches with a \nflash flooding potential depending on the area. An isolated \nstrong wind gust threat will also accompany with any \nthunderstorm.\n\nPrecipitable water comes down a bit on Tuesday as mean flow\nbecomes northeasterly. Thunderstorms should form off the high \nterrain and the Mogollon Rim, moving west/southwest with an\nisolated flash flooding or strong wind threat.\n\nDaily chances for thunderstorms will continue through the week.\nThere remains a signal for an easterly wave to make its way into\nthe region during the second half of the work week, which would\nincrease atmospheric moisture content and shower/thunderstorm \nchances. Details will need to be worked out about flooding/severity\npotential however as the degree instability is still uncertain.\nAs a final note, the displacement of the upper ridge and \nincoming easterly wave will transition forecast high \ntemperatures from a couple degrees below normal through mid- \nweek, to potentially 5-8 degrees below normal or even lower \ndepending on cloud cover and rain Friday into the weekend.",
